\viewkind4\uc1\pard\sa160\sl252\slmult1\i\f0\fs22 Waste Management Recycling Education Representative Bobby Bell reported that Manhattan Beach experienced its first truck fire caused by a battery, which was quickly extinguished by the Fire Department; shared that Waste Management hosted a story time at the library featuring books about trash trucks, where children actively participated and had the opportunity to see a trash truck in person; and responded to City Council questions.\line\line Library Manager Cassandra Sargent announced the upcoming \ldblquote Explore Dance\rdblquote  performance featuring Benita Bike, scheduled for Saturday, February 28, 2026, from 2:00 PM to 3:00 PM.\line\line Manhattan Beach resident Mike Jenkins announced, in collaboration with the Mira Costa High School Black Scholars Union, Pacific Islanders Club, Latino Scholars Union, St. Michael\rquote s Club, Middle Eastern North African (MENA) Club, Jewish Cultural Club, Mira Costa Jazz Band, and Mira Costa Choir, will host a multicultural celebration at the Joslyn Community Center on Sunday, February 22, 2026, from 2:00 to 4:00 PM.\line\line Police Chief Rachel Johnson invited the community to attend the Police and Fire Departments\rquote  Town Hall Meeting at the Joslyn Community Center on Wednesday, February 18, 2026, from 6:00 PM to 8:00 PM, and also encouraged participation in Coffee with a Cop at Dunkin\rquote  Donuts on Thursday, February 26, 2026, from 8:00 AM to 10:00 AM.\line\line Financial Services Manager Emy-Rose Hanna announced that the City\rquote s budget survey is currently open to the public and will close on February 28, 2026. She noted that the survey results will be shared during the proposed budget discussion scheduled for May 2026.\line\line Manhattan Beach Little League Vice President Craig Snyder announced that the Little League Carnival and Parade will return this weekend at American Martyrs School on Saturday, February 21, 2026, from 9:00 AM to 2:00 PM, and responded to City Council questions.\line\line Councilmember Charelian also announced that the Manhattan Beach Little League Opening Day Parade and Carnival will take place on Saturday, February 21, 2026, from 9:00 a.m. to 2:00 p.m. He expressed appreciation to the Police, Fire, and Public Works Departments for their efforts in organizing the event.\line\line Councilmember Tarnay recognized and celebrated Lunar New Year, a time of renewal, reflection, and hope. She noted that the Year of the Horse symbolizes energy, determination, resilience, and a strong spirit of independence.\line\line\pard Mayor Lesser invited the community to attend Coffee with the Mayor at Metlox Plaza on Friday, March 6, 2026, from 9:00 AM to 10:00 AM.\cf1\i0\f1\par
}


